# ~/.oracle-monitor/config — Oracle Monitor Configuration (macOS)
#
# This file is sourced by oracle-monitor-macos.sh (v2.5.4-macos.1+).
# Edit values below to override the built-in defaults.
# Lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# After editing, verify with:
#   ./oracle-monitor-macos.sh --dry-run
#
# For dual-instance (e.g. testnet + mainnet on one Mac):
#   ./oracle-monitor-macos.sh --config /path/to/config --dry-run
#
# Docs: https://github.com/BaumerCrypto/digidollar-oracle-tools

# ---- Discord Webhook (required for alerts) ----
# Get from: Server Settings > Integrations > Webhooks > Copy URL
DISCORD_WEBHOOK="https://discord.com/api/webhooks/YOUR_WEBHOOK_ID/YOUR_WEBHOOK_TOKEN"

# ---- Oracle Identity ----
ORACLE_ID=0
ORACLE_NAME="my-oracle"

# ---- RPC Settings ----
# If digibyte-cli isn't on your PATH, use its full path, e.g.:
#   CLI="/Users/YOURNAME/digibyte/bin/digibyte-cli -testnet"
# Testnet:  CLI="digibyte-cli -testnet"
# Mainnet:  CLI="digibyte-cli"
# Dual-instance mainnet with conf file:
#   CLI="digibyte-cli -conf=$HOME/Library/Application Support/DigiByte/mainnet.conf"
CLI="digibyte-cli -testnet"
WALLET_FLAG="-rpcwallet=oracle"

# ---- Daemon Process Name (v2.5.2-macos.1+) ----
# The monitor auto-detects either "digibyted" (headless daemon) or the
# Qt GUI wallet. On macOS the Qt process name has never been standardized
# across releases, so auto-detect tries THREE Qt candidates in order:
#   1. DigiByte-Qt   (matches .app bundle capitalization on recent builds)
#   2. Digibyte-Qt   (lower-case 'b' — some older releases)
#   3. digibyte-qt   (Linux-style all-lowercase — cross-compiled builds)
# In auto-detect mode digibyted (headless) is tried first, then the three
# Qt candidates. The first pgrep -x match wins and the actual matched name
# appears in the Discord "Node: X running" line.
#
# Uncomment ONE line below to force a specific match. Only needed if you
# run BOTH binaries on the same Mac and want to pin monitoring to one.
#
# For Qt-wallet operators: leave commented out — auto-detect will find it.
# The launchd check (Check #8) automatically skips with an INFO line when
# any Qt candidate is the detected daemon, since Qt usually runs outside
# launchd.
#
# To verify what your Qt binary shows up as:
#   pgrep -x DigiByte-Qt || pgrep -x Digibyte-Qt || pgrep -x digibyte-qt
#
# DAEMON_PROCESS="digibyted"
# DAEMON_PROCESS="DigiByte-Qt"
# DAEMON_PROCESS="Digibyte-Qt"
# DAEMON_PROCESS="digibyte-qt"

# ---- launchd Label (optional) ----
# If you run digibyted via a LaunchAgent, set the label here and Check #8
# will report its loaded/not-loaded state. Ignored automatically when a
# Qt wallet is the detected daemon (v2.5.2-macos.1+).
#   LAUNCHD_LABEL="org.digibyte.digibyted"
LAUNCHD_LABEL=""

# ---- Disk Watch Path ----
# The volume containing this path is watched for free space. The default
# DigiByte datadir on macOS is ~/Library/Application Support/DigiByte,
# which lives on the same volume as $HOME on most Macs — the default is
# fine unless your datadir is on an external drive.
DISK_PATH="${HOME}"

# ---- DigiByte Datadir Named in Low Disk Alerts (v2.5.5-macos.1+) ----
# The Low Disk Space alert prints this path on its own line so you know
# exactly where to clean up. Display-only — the monitor never reads or
# deletes anything here. No RPC returns the datadir, so it's declared in
# config. Set it per config file, same pattern as NETWORK_LABEL:
#
#   Single-instance (one daemon on this Mac) — leave the default:
#     DATADIR="$HOME/Library/Application Support/DigiByte"
#
#   Dual-instance (testnet + mainnet via --config) — set each config so
#   each instance's alert names its own datadir:
#     testnet config:  DATADIR="$HOME/Library/Application Support/DigiByte/testnet26"
#     mainnet config:  DATADIR="$HOME/Library/Application Support/DigiByte"
#
# Mainnet chain data lives at the datadir top level; testnet data lives
# in a subdirectory named for the current testnet reset (testnet26,
# testnet27, ...) — bump it when the testnet resets.
DATADIR="${HOME}/Library/Application Support/DigiByte"

# ---- Alert Thresholds ----
MIN_PEERS=3             # Minimum peer count before alerting
MIN_DISK_GB=5           # Minimum free disk space (GB) before alerting
STALE_PRICE_MINUTES=30  # Reserved for future use — staleness currently from RPC
MEM_THRESHOLD=90        # Memory usage % above which to alert
MAX_CHAIN_BEHIND=10     # Blocks behind before alerting

# ---- Swap Pressure Threshold (v2.4-macos.1+) ----
# Alert when system-wide swap usage exceeds this many MB.
#
# macOS uses a dynamic swap file that grows on demand — sysctl vm.swapusage
# reports its current total and used size. On a healthy Mac with plenty of
# RAM, swap stays near 0 MB. Any sustained non-trivial usage means the
# system is fighting for memory.
#
# Recommended values:
#   Desktop / plenty of RAM (16GB+):     100 MB
#   Laptop with 8GB and browser tabs:    500 MB
#   Dual-daemon (testnet + mainnet):     1500 MB
#
# To calibrate: after 24h of stable operation, run:
#   sysctl vm.swapusage
# and set the threshold ~200-500 MB above the "used" value you see.
SWAP_THRESHOLD_MB=100

# ---- NTP Check ----
# One `sntp` query measures your real clock offset against this server.
# Offsets beyond NTP_MAX_OFFSET trigger a desync warning. Oracle bundle
# timestamps are rejected past 3600s skew, so keep this tight — a drifting
# clock kills your signing.
NTP_SERVER="time.apple.com"
NTP_MAX_OFFSET="1.0"

# ---- Quorum Alert Bands (v2.0, tuned in v2.5.1) ----
# Band thresholds for network-wide oracle liveness.
# The on-chain minimum (oracle_consensus_required) comes from the chain
# via getdigidollardeploymentinfo — not configurable here (currently 7-of-35).
#
# Recommended values (v2.5.1):
#   Mainnet (production, 35-slot roster):  GREEN=12, YELLOW=10
#   Testnet26 (smaller active population): GREEN=10, YELLOW=8
#
# The v2.0-v2.4 defaults (20/12) were too permissive and fired "getting thin"
# at 15/35 fresh — more than 2x the hard 7-of-35 quorum floor. This
# conditioned operators to ignore quorum warnings entirely. New defaults
# fire only at meaningful risk levels.
QUORUM_GREEN=12         # At or above = comfortable (no alerts)
QUORUM_YELLOW=10        # At or above but below green = "getting thin"

# ---- Anti-Flap (v2.1) ----
# Cooldown: minimum minutes between quorum RECOVERY alerts.
# Escalation (getting worse) always fires immediately.
# Set to 0 to disable (v2.0 behavior = alert every state change).
QUORUM_COOLDOWN=30

# Hysteresis: recovery requires exceeding the threshold by this buffer.
# Example: GREEN=12, HYSTERESIS=3 → must hit 15 to recover to green.
# Prevents rapid green/yellow flapping when count hovers near boundary.
# Set to 0 to disable (v2.0 behavior = recover at exact threshold).
QUORUM_HYSTERESIS=3

# ---- Network Label (v2.5.1-macos.1+) ----
# Identifies which chain this monitor instance is watching. Appears in
# Discord card titles and the --dry-run / --watch / --test output:
#   "✅ All Systems Healthy — Testnet26 Health Summary"
#   "⚠️ 1 Warnings — Mainnet Health Summary"
#   "🔧 Mainnet Test Alert"
#   "🔭 Mainnet Monitor — watch mode (refreshes every 60s, Ctrl+C to exit)"
#
# Leave commented out for the generic "Oracle" label.
# NETWORK_LABEL="Testnet26"
# NETWORK_LABEL="Mainnet"

# ---- Dual-Instance Monitoring (v2.3-macos.1+) ----
# oracle-monitor-macos.sh supports --config /path to run against a separate
# config file. This lets you monitor testnet and mainnet from one Mac
# without running two copies of the script.
#
# How it works:
#   - Each instance reads its own config file (CLI path, webhook, thresholds)
#   - State files (quorum_state, daemon_down, etc.) auto-separate by config
#     directory — the state dir is derived from `dirname CONFIG_FILE`
#
# Setup example — adding a mainnet instance alongside testnet:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.oracle-monitor-mainnet
#   cp ~/.oracle-monitor/config ~/.oracle-monitor-mainnet/config
#
#   # Edit the mainnet config to override at least:
#   #   CLI="digibyte-cli"                          (no -testnet flag)
#   #   SWAP_THRESHOLD_MB=1500                      (dual-daemon baseline)
#   #   QUORUM_GREEN=12, QUORUM_YELLOW=10           (tighter for production)
#   #   NETWORK_LABEL="Mainnet"                     (shows in card titles)
#   #   DATADIR="$HOME/Library/Application Support/DigiByte"
#   #                                               (named in Low Disk alerts —
#   #                                               and set the TESTNET config to
#   #                                               the .../DigiByte/testnet26 subdir)
#   #   DISCORD_WEBHOOK="https://..."               (same or different webhook)
#
#   # Test:
#   ./oracle-monitor-macos.sh --config ~/.oracle-monitor-mainnet/config --dry-run
#
#   # Add to cron (offset by 2 minutes to avoid RPC collision with testnet):
#   2-59/5 * * * * /Users/YOUR_USER/oracle-monitor-macos.sh --config /Users/YOUR_USER/.oracle-monitor-mainnet/config 2>/dev/null
#   0 1,13 * * * /Users/YOUR_USER/oracle-monitor-macos.sh --config /Users/YOUR_USER/.oracle-monitor-mainnet/config --summary 2>/dev/null
